## Reality check

{% block icon="eye" %}
The app started as an initiative from the engineers, in collaboration with INSO colleagues from the country offices, who had concluded a few things that pointed towards a mobile app:
{% /block %}

- SMS was getting more expensive, which put pressure on country office budgets.
- Internet coverage was increasing, and so was smartphone use: the average NGO worker carries one wherever they are, alongside other communication devices if needed.

Those sessions were the only research behind the app, and the engineers had mostly focused on the technical side. I took the project on wanting to do further research, but with those sessions done, a demo already implemented and two other projects in my pipeline, {% mark text="I decided to focus on design principles instead" /%}. So I based the redesign on a heuristic evaluation and testing with country office colleagues, knowing there would be no baseline to compare against.

{% cut end=true /%}

## Where it started

**The app existed, but wasn't made for the people who could benefit from it**

The idea was simple: take alerts out of SMS and email and put them in one place, with a map, filters and updates people can follow on the move.

My way in was pure curiosity after hearing the developers talk about it for a while. One look and my admittedly biased designer brain went "what is this, and who is it for?" There was no INSO blue (INSO's main brand colour) anywhere, brown and beige were occupying the majority of the design, and the screens felt built for engineers more than for someone reading an alert under pressure.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-former-version.jpg" frame="before" alt="The app when I joined: a dense dark list of alerts in capitals, an alert detail packed with text under a small map, and a map that shows a single alert pin." caption="Visuals showing what the App looked like when I joined the project team." /%}

{% block icon="testing" title="The solution came before the problem" %}
There was no problem statement to design against, so a quick heuristic evaluation of the app became my starting point. After sharing it with the project lead, I asked to take the app on, with the idea of lining up the UI with the brand, not knowing it would grow into a UX redesign. Along the way it felt like a patchwork every now and then.
{% /block %}

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-2.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-3.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-4.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-5.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-6.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-7.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/product-8.jpg" alts="The alerts list.|Filters: programmes and alerts with a location only.|The date picker with quick options.|The list, filtered.|The map in Explore mode.|An alert opened from the map.|Proximity: a radius around a place.|Geofence: an area drawn on the map." caption="High-level overview of the app structure and key interactions around alerts." /%}

## Users

**Three groups, one sending and two receiving**

**NGO field workers and security staff** receive the alerts and need them fast and clear, often under pressure.

**INSO colleagues in the country offices** write and send them, mostly from laptops through the Partners Portal.

There was no direct access to NGO workers before launch, so country office colleagues stood in for them, through interviews and remote prototype tests in Maze built around real scenarios. Partners only weighed in after launch, through a survey.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-users.jpg" alt="Board in three columns: who (NGO field workers, security and operations staff, INSO field monitors), their key needs, and the product and real-world pain points." caption="Breakdown of the different user profiles, their needs and pain points." /%}

## Login

**Users were asked to act before understanding what the app does**

A quick heuristic evaluation of the startup and login screen turned up a few serious problems. The app opened straight on a login form and asked for location access before anyone had logged in, with no hint of why. It never said what the app was for. The login itself was hard to use too, with a barely visible button, an "Invalid email" error before anyone had typed, how to get an account explained in a tiny note, and no way to reset a password. Country office colleagues believed a welcome email would be enough to explain things, but someone could install the app today and only open it a month later when an alert comes in, and by then that email is long gone. So the explanation had to live inside the app.

{% reactions set="inso-login" /%}

{% block icon="did" title="Explain first, ask for location later" %}
Here is how I approached each of the main problems from my heuristic evaluation of the startup and login screen.
{% /block %}

| Problem | Solution |
|---|---|
| Location access was asked first, before anyone had logged in and with no explanation | Location access is now asked only after, once people know what it's for |
| The app never said what it was for | Two short onboarding screens now come before the login |
| The login button was barely visible, and "Invalid email" showed before anyone had typed | The login got the brand back. It starts with just an email and a clear Continue button, and the validation stopped shouting "Invalid email" before anyone had typed a thing |
| How to get an account was a tiny note, and there was no way to reset a password | "Don't have an account? Learn more" now sits under the form in readable text and leads to the registration page on ngosafety.org, with a "Forgot password?" link and a show/hide toggle on the password |

{% cut note="Repeats the table above." /%}

Two short onboarding screens now come before the login, and location access is asked only after, once people know what it's for. The login got the brand back, and the validation stopped shouting "Invalid email" before anyone had typed a thing.

{% cut end=true /%}

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-onboarding-02.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-onboarding-03.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-onboarding-04.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-onboarding-05.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-onboarding-06.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-login-01.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/onboarding-login/inso-app-login-02.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The INSO splash screen.|Onboarding, 1 of 2: stay informed and safe while on the move.|Onboarding, 1 of 2, ready to move on.|Onboarding, 2 of 2: monitor any location remotely.|Onboarding, 2 of 2, in the dark map style.|The new login: Let's get started, with an email field and a Continue button.|The login's second step: email and password, a show/hide toggle, Log in and Forgot password." captions="The app opens on the INSO logo, and two short onboarding screens come before the login.|Two short onboarding screens now come before the login: what the app is for.|Two short onboarding screens now come before the login: what the app is for.|The second onboarding screen: monitoring a place you're not in.|The second onboarding screen: monitoring a place you're not in.|The login got the brand back: just an email and a clear Continue button, and \"Don't have an account? Learn more\" in readable text under the form.|Then the password, with a show/hide toggle and a \"Forgot password?\" link." /%}

